    "created_at": "2022-11-25T20:16:31Z",
    "updated_at": "2022-11-25T20:16:31Z",
    "body": "It's not a pure alias in Blink either:\r\n\r\n```js\r\nvar {style} = document.createElement(\"div\");\r\nstyle.cssText = \"-webkit-border-radius: 1px 2px\";\r\nstyle.cssText; // 'border-radius: 1px / 2px;'\r\n```\r\n\r\n> This implies one, three, and four length values are identical?\r\n\r\nYes, the grammar of `-webkit-border-radius` is identical to `border-radius` except when exactly two space-separated values are provided. See parser code for [Blink](https://source.chromium.org/chromium/chromium/src/+/main:third_party/blink/renderer/core/css/properties/css_parsing_utils.cc;drc=a9d8d3650ef6c2cf0a58de2db9621f8c647d8e58;l=5783) and [WebKit](https://searchfox.org/wubkat/rev/70230d10f272f17151f1e77d496553716b449cea/Source/WebCore/css/parser/CSSPropertyParserHelpers.cpp#7096).\r\n\r\nBTW, this implies that serializing `-webkit-border-radius` for `border-radius: 1px 2px` should not be `1px 2px` in Blink/WebKit. Most reasonable candidates are `1px 2px 1px`, `1px 2px 1px 2px` and `1px 2px / 1px 2px`, probably it should be the 1st one which is the shortest one. But Blink uses the wrong `1px 2px`, and WebKit never serializes `-webkit-border-radius`. ",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
